%I A399894 #11 Sep 23 2026 23:43:39
%S A399894 1,3,5,7,10,13,15,17,20,22
%N A399894 Maximum number of points that can be chosen from an n X n grid so that no four of them form an isosceles trapezium (rectangles included) and no four lie on a common line.
%C A399894 Defined by Ghosal et al. (2026), Appendix B, as a proxy for the no-four-on-a-circle problem (A395821), since almost all cyclic quadrilaterals in the grid are isosceles trapezia. For a set with no four collinear points the condition is equivalent to all perpendicular bisectors of pairs of points being distinct.
%C A399894 A395821(n) <= a(n) <= A072567(n), since every isosceles trapezium is a cyclic quadrilateral and every axis-parallel rectangle is an isosceles trapezium. Also a(n) <= floor((5n-3)/2) (Thiele's argument).
%C A399894 Ghosal et al. give lower bounds for n <= 30 (Appendix B): 1, 3, 5, 7, 10, 13, 15, 17, 20, 22, 24, 27, 29, 32, 34, 36, 38, 40, 43, 45, 47, 49, 51, 54, 55, 58, 60, 62, 64, 66. These are attained for n <= 10. Terms for n = 2..10 were computed by exhaustive SAT search. a(11) >= 24.
%H A399894 A. Ghosal, R. Goenka, A. Grebennikov, P. Keevash, M. Kwan, and H. T. Pham, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05255">No-(k+1)-in-line problem for k >= 3</a>, arXiv:2607.05255 [math.CO], 2026. See Appendix B.
%H A399894 Azmi C. Özgen, <a href="/A399894/a399894.txt">Python program</a>
%H A399894 T. Thiele, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(95)90007-1">The no-four-on-circle problem</a>, J. Combin. Theory Ser. A 71 (1995), 332-334.
%e A399894 For n = 5 the ten points (0,3), (1,0), (1,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,3), (2,4), (3,0), (4,0), (4,4) contain no isosceles trapezium and no four collinear points (verified with the checker in the certificate bundle).
%Y A399894 Cf. A072567, A227133, A271906, A271907, A272651, A395821.
